## How to Verify a Yellow Gold Marquise Before You Buy

Three checks every ring must pass before you commit.

**Check 1 — Confirm the centre stone colour grade matches the Yellow Gold Rule.** H colour for sweet spot, I for high value, J for max savings. Avoid F or G colour in yellow gold — you are paying for white-metal grade in a warm-metal setting that doesn't need it.

**Check 2 — Confirm V-tip centre prongs at order.** Yellow gold does not change the tip chip risk. V-tip mandatory. $0 extra on most listings.

**Check 3 — Confirm metal purity (14k or 18k).** 14k is harder and better for daily wear. 18k is richer in colour but slightly softer. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) and [GIA 4Cs](https://4cs.gia.edu/) verify loose stones only — the metal purity is on the retailer's listing.

I run these three checks before I quote any client on a yellow gold marquise. All three must pass for the ring to deliver on the colour-savings strategy.

## Recent Update — What Changed in This Guide

Updated {{month_year}} with current certified market inventory for yellow gold marquise settings — 49 settings mapped from $860 to $4,750 across every style (solitaire, cathedral, halo, hidden halo, three-stone, pavé, bezel, vintage, east-west). Added the 4-Grade Yellow Gold Rule, the Warm-Metal Color Masking Effect, and the Marquise Belly Color Pool framework after reviewing 100+ yellow gold vs white gold colour-comparison sessions with clients over 2 years. The consistent finding is that H colour in yellow gold reads identical to F colour in white gold at normal viewing distances. Our pick remains the Petite Solitaire in 14k Yellow Gold at $1,000 for daily wear + the Tapered Cathedral Solitaire at $1,240 for cathedral buyers.

